dotnet_list_vulnerable.json — from documented schema — verify on first real run.

Hand-built, not captured: no .NET SDK is installed on the machine this was written on
(2026-09-19). The shape is NuGet's own, read the same day from the renderer that writes it and
the test that pins its output, both on NuGet.Client's `dev` branch (commit 1e41905, 2026-09-19):

    src/NuGet.Core/NuGet.CommandLine.XPlat/ListPackage/ListPackageJsonRenderer.cs
        — the property-name constants (`version`, `parameters`, `sources`, `projects`, `path`,
          `frameworks`, `framework`, `topLevelPackages`, `transitivePackages`, `id`,
          `requestedVersion`, `resolvedVersion`, `vulnerabilities`, `severity`, `advisoryurl`,
          `problems`, `level`, `text`) and WriteVulnerabilities, which maps severity 0..3 to
          "Low", "Moderate", "High", "Critical" and anything else to "".
    test/NuGet.Core.FuncTests/NuGet.XPlat.FuncTest/XplatListPackageJsonRendererTests.cs
        — JsonRenderer_ListPackage_Vulnerable_IncludeTransitive_SucceedsAsync: the expected
          document this fixture's first framework block is copied from (package ids A and D,
          those three GHSA ids, the `[1.0.0,1.3.0]` requested range), with the test's
          netcoreapp3.1 renamed to net8.0.

The second framework block (net9.0) is a copy of the first. That is the repeat the real command
produces for a multi-targeted project — one entry per target framework, and again per project
in a solution — and it is why `langs/csharp.py`'s `audit_findings` dedupes on
(id, resolvedVersion, advisoryurl): the fixture should parse to three lines, not six.

Not exercised here, covered inline in test_lang_csharp.py: a `problems` entry of level
"error" (the only thing that makes the command exit 1 — ListPackageCommandRunner.cs computes
the exit code from ProblemType.Error alone; vulnerable packages never set it).

Verify against a real `dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive --format json` the
first time one runs, and replace this file with the capture.
